    fullStory: [
      `Ntozake Shange was born Paulette Williams in Trenton, New Jersey, in 1948, into a family where art, education, and Black intellectual life mattered. As a child, she encountered major Black artists and thinkers through her parents' circles. That early exposure did not make her path easy, but it meant she grew up knowing Black creativity was not marginal. It was central.`,
      `At Barnard College and later in graduate study, Shange came of age during Black student organizing, feminism, antiwar politics, and the aftermath of the Black Arts Movement. She also lived through depression and a painful period of personal crisis. Her work did not treat survival as a neat inspirational arc. It asked what language, movement, and community could do when ordinary speech failed.`,
      `The name Ntozake Shange marked a self-remaking. Naming was not costume. It was part of a broader rejection of forms that had made Black women legible only through other people's categories. Her writing changed spelling, punctuation, line breaks, and grammar to create sound and motion on the page. Some readers saw difficulty. Shange was making English answer to Black speech, rhythm, and embodiment.`,
      `for colored girls who have considered suicide / when the rainbow is enuf grew from performances rather than from a conventional play blueprint. Seven women identified by colors speak, move, remember, joke, mourn, and testify. The work includes sexual violence, abortion, abandonment, friendship, music, and the famous image of reclaiming the "stuff" of self. It is not a lecture about Black women. It is a stage space where Black women speak to and through one another.`,
      `Calling it a choreopoem mattered. Shange needed a form where poem and body were inseparable. Dance was not decoration between speeches. Movement carried meaning. Music was not background. It shaped memory. The form insisted that Black women's interior lives could be theatrical architecture, not subplot.`,
      `The Broadway success of for colored girls was historic, but success brought argument. Some Black men criticized the work as damaging or exposing private pain. Some critics did not know how to read its language or form. Shange did not write to make the community look respectable to outsiders. She wrote from the fact that silence could be another wound.`,
      `That argument over exposure was not new. Black artists have often been asked to protect the race from white judgment by keeping certain injuries private. Shange rejected the bargain. The pain in for colored girls is not presented for voyeurism when staged responsibly. It is held inside a circle of women whose speech, movement, and color create their own terms of witness. The audience is allowed to listen, not to own the story.`,
      `Her career continued beyond that landmark. She wrote novels, essays, children's books, poetry, and plays. Sassafrass, Cypress & Indigo explored sisters, art, food, magic, family, and Black women's creative inheritance. Other works turned to history, diaspora, and everyday ritual. Food, color, and music often mattered because they were ways of knowing, not decorative details.`,
      `Shange also changed what later artists could attempt. Spoken-word performance, Black feminist theater, hybrid memoir, dance-theater, and ensemble pieces about women's interior lives all move more freely because she made a major stage accept a form that had not been waiting in the wings. The door she opened was formal as much as representational.`,
      `Shange's legacy is strongest when taught as formal innovation and not only as representation. She did not merely put Black women onstage. She changed what a stage could do for Black women's language, bodies, and memory. She taught readers and audiences that survival may require a new grammar.`
    ],
