    fullStory: [
      `Pauli Murray was born in Baltimore in 1910 and raised in Durham, North Carolina, by relatives after family tragedy. That early life placed Murray inside the Black South, the church, education, and the daily humiliations of segregation. Murray was brilliant, restless, funny, exacting, and often forced to build a path where no institution had made one.`,
      `Murray's education was a sequence of barriers and refusals. Murray wanted to attend the University of North Carolina for graduate study in the 1930s, but the school rejected Murray because of race. Murray later attended Howard University School of Law, where sexism inside a Black institution produced another lesson. Murray called the combination of racism and sexism "Jane Crow," a phrase that named what many Black women and gender-nonconforming people experienced when movements treated race and gender as separate problems.`,
      `In 1940, Murray and Adelene McBean were arrested in Virginia after refusing to accept segregated bus seating. The incident came more than a decade before Rosa Parks's famous arrest. Murray's activism did not become the mass-movement symbol, but it belonged to the same tradition of testing segregation through law, courage, and the refusal to cooperate.`,
      `At Howard, Murray graduated first in the law school class. Harvard Law School denied Murray admission to a graduate program because Harvard did not admit women. Murray instead earned a master's degree at the University of California, Berkeley. The repeated exclusions did not stop Murray from producing work other lawyers needed. States' Laws on Race and Color, published in 1950, cataloged segregation law so thoroughly that Thurgood Marshall reportedly called it a bible for civil rights lawyers.`,
      `That book mattered because legal movements need maps. Segregation was not one law waiting to be knocked down. It was a maze of statutes, local rules, customs, and enforcement practices. Murray's compilation made the maze visible. Civil rights lawyers could use it to see patterns, contradictions, and targets. The work was painstaking, not glamorous, and exactly the kind of intellectual labor that later landmark cases require.`,
      `Murray's legal imagination was ahead of institutional timing. Murray argued that the Fourteenth Amendment could be used not only against racial discrimination but also against sex discrimination. That thinking later influenced feminist legal strategy. In Reed v. Reed, Ruth Bader Ginsburg and colleagues listed Murray as a coauthor on a brief even though Murray did not draft it, acknowledging the debt to Murray's work.`,
      `Murray's life also requires care around gender. Murray used different names and sought medical help related to a persistent sense of being male or not fitting the gender assigned at birth. Historical sources use varied language. Some contemporary interpreters describe Murray as transgender or nonbinary. Others use the pronouns Murray used publicly in life. A responsible page should not pretend certainty where the archive is complex, but it should not erase the evidence that gender was a serious and painful part of Murray's life.`,
      `In 1977, Murray became an Episcopal priest, adding another chapter to a life already full of law, poetry, activism, and teaching. Ministry was not an escape from justice work. It was another form of it. Murray preached and served while carrying the history of a person who had pushed law, church, and movements to see more clearly.`,
      `Pauli Murray matters because the footnotes changed the law. Murray's arguments helped shape civil rights and feminist legal thought, but recognition came slowly. The fuller story is not only about a hidden genius finally found. It is about why institutions repeatedly needed Murray's mind while refusing Murray full belonging.`
    ],
    quote: {
      text: 'One person plus one typewriter constitutes a movement.',
      cite: 'Pauli Murray, quoted by the Pauli Murray Center'
    },
    costImpact: [
      `Murray paid for being early. Racial exclusion, sexism, employment discrimination, gender distress, and institutional rejection shaped Murray's life. Murray's ideas were often useful to movements before Murray was fully welcomed by them. That is a particular kind of cost: to help build the argument and still stand partly outside the room.`,
      `The impact is visible in civil rights law, feminist legal theory, Black feminist thought, LGBTQ historical memory, and Episcopal history. Murray gave later generations language and legal strategy for problems that institutions wanted to separate. The phrase Jane Crow still names the cost of being asked to split oneself into categories that power can manage.`
    ],
    whyItMattersToday: [
      `Pauli Murray matters today because law often changes through arguments that first sound impossible. Murray saw connections between race, sex, labor, and citizenship before courts and movements were ready to act on them. That kind of thinking is not abstract. It changes briefs, statutes, classrooms, and lives.`,
      `Murray's story also teaches care in historical language. Modern readers may recognize gender experiences in Murray's archive, but the vocabulary of Murray's lifetime was different and often hostile. Respect means neither erasing that evidence nor forcing it into certainty for our convenience.`,
      `The fuller lesson is that movements need people who see across walls. Murray saw across them, named them, and left maps for people who came later.`
    ],
